In many ways, yes. Money is stored optionality. It’s the ability to choose rather than merely react to circumstances.

It buys more than things. It buys time, mobility, privacy, independence, the ability to say no, and the ability to take risks without putting everything you love in jeopardy.

And there’s a deeper connection to what you were saying about performing in the eyes of others:

Financial independence reduces the power other people have to dictate your behavior.

You can walk away from a bad employer. Refuse a deal. Pursue something unconventional. Spend Tuesday afternoon with your children. Choose your own standards of success.

But money and freedom aren’t identical. Someone with $10 million can be imprisoned by expectations, lifestyle inflation, fear, or ambition. Someone with much less can have substantial autonomy.

I’d distinguish three levels:

* Security: Enough money that survival doesn’t dictate your decisions.
* Independence: Enough assets or reliable income that you control your time.
* Sovereignty: Enough freedom, discipline, and self-knowledge to decide what your life is actually for.

The third isn’t something money can purchase outright, but money can make it considerably easier to exercise.

And perhaps the greatest irony is that people sometimes sacrifice their freedom to accumulate the money they believe will eventually buy it back.

The goal isn’t necessarily to become rich. It’s to become increasingly difficult to coerce, increasingly free to create, and increasingly able to give your time to what you love.

Which raises an interesting question: How much money would actually be enough to stop organizing your life around acquiring more of it?